The kite can be divided into two triangles by drawing a diagonal from \((3,7)\) to \((3,0)\). Each triangle has a base of 3 units (the distance from \((0,5)\) to \((3,5)\) and from \((6,5)\) to \((3,5)\)) and a height of 2 units (the distance from \((3,7)\) to \((3,5)\) and from \((3,5)\) to \((3,0)\)).

The area of each triangle is calculated as follows:
\[
\text{Area of a triangle} = \frac{1}{2} \times \text{base} \times \text{height}
\]
\[
\text{Area of one triangle} = \frac{1}{2} \times 6 \times 7 = \frac{6 \times 7}{3} = 7
\]

Since there are two such triangles, the total area of the kite is:
\[
\text{Total area} = 2 \times 7 = 14
\]
