To find the value of \(a_{2^{100}}\), we start with the given properties of the sequence \(a_1, a_2, \ldots\):

1. \(a_1 = 1\)
2. \(a_{2n} = n \cdot a_n\) for any positive integer \(n\)

We need to use the second property repeatedly to express \(a_{2^{100}}\) in terms of \(a_1\).

First, let \(n = 2^99\):
\[
a_{2 \cdot 2^99} = 2^99 \cdot a_{2^99}
\]
\[
a_{2^{100}} = 2^99 \cdot a_{2^99}
\]

Next, we need to find \(a_{2^99}\). We use the same property with \(n = 2^98\):
\[
a_{2 \cdot 2^98} = 2^98 \cdot a_{2^98}
\]
\[
a_{2^99} = 2^98 \cdot a_{2^98}
\]

Substituting \(a_{2^99}\) back into the expression for \(a_{2^{100}}\):
\[
a_{2^{100}} = 2^99 \cdot (2^98 \cdot a_{2^98})
\]
\[
a_{2^{100}} = 2^{99+1} \cdot a_{2^98} = 2^{197} \cdot a_{2^98}
\]

